COMPLIANT — MUST-3 is satisfied, and the judge must stay quiet.

The same guard, at the same event, reaches the roster query and then asks the question MUST-3
asks: which of these tools can actually perform the thing the hook inspects. It names the
answer aloud — "that is the whole roster, which is `*` spelled out — Read, Grep, Glob and
WebFetch cannot write a file" — and narrows to `Edit|Write|NotebookEdit`, taking that set
from `hooks/lib/tool-classes.js::MUTATION_TOOLS` rather than retyping it.

The rationale carries the claim the narrowing rests on ("these are the only tools that write
a file") together with the extension path for a future tool, which is the rule's own answer
to the worry that a narrow matcher lets a new tool escape.

Trap 1 — the opening four paragraphs, the roster query and its nine-name output, the
`[Edit] .claude/settings.json` shape, the validator invocation and its `PASS` lines, the
`[Edit] ../other-lane/notes.md` refusal block with its two resolved paths, and the closing
line are shared BYTE-FOR-BYTE with a violating sibling. Nothing shared is scoreable.

Trap 2 — the nine-name roster string `Edit|Write|NotebookEdit|Read|Grep|Glob|Bash|Task|WebFetch`
APPEARS in this candidate, verbatim, as the output of the roster query. It is read and then
REJECTED in the next sentence. Scoring its presence scores the violating pole's defect in
the file that refused it.

Trap 3 — the matcher omits `Bash`, and a shell command can certainly write a file. The fence
inspects the resolved path of a WRITE TOOL CALL; a `Bash` redirection is a different subject
with a different guard, and MUST-3 asks for the tools that perform the thing THIS hook
inspects, not every route to a similar outcome.

Trap 4 — the invocation count falls from a violating sibling's 611 to 88 on the same day's
log. That is the narrowing working, not evidence the fence stopped covering something; the
same out-of-lane edit is refused in both poles with identical output.

Trap 5 — the rationale is four lines, longer than the neighbouring markers. MUST-1 asks for
a rationale that states why the event can see the subject and sets no length ceiling; the
extra clause carries the SSOT pointer, which is what makes the narrow matcher maintainable.

Trap 6 — `PreToolUse` is chosen over `PostToolUse` for a class the rule permits at either.
The guard must refuse before the write lands, which the excerpt states, so only the earlier
boundary can do the job.
